1
00:00:08,300 --> 00:00:11,285
Hello. This time,

2
00:00:11,285 --> 00:00:14,435
we're going to talk about
quality of service.

3
00:00:14,435 --> 00:00:17,260
Quality of service refers to

4
00:00:17,260 --> 00:00:20,605
the overall performance
of a service.

5
00:00:20,605 --> 00:00:25,200
What my applications care
about, things like bandwidth,

6
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:26,920
making sure that they get enough

7
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:29,800
bandwidth that their
applications need.

8
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:32,175
If you're streaming a movie,

9
00:00:32,175 --> 00:00:35,170
you want to make sure that
you have enough bandwidth.

10
00:00:35,170 --> 00:00:38,150
Latency, how long

11
00:00:38,150 --> 00:00:41,540
a packet takes to get from
one point to another.

12
00:00:41,540 --> 00:00:43,850
Jitter, this is

13
00:00:43,850 --> 00:00:47,410
the difference effectively
in the latency.

14
00:00:47,410 --> 00:00:51,220
You want it to be a
lot more regular,

15
00:00:51,220 --> 00:00:53,585
the packet arrival so

16
00:00:53,585 --> 00:00:57,590
you can have good call quality
or something like that.

17
00:00:57,590 --> 00:01:00,410
Then the last maybe is loss.

18
00:01:00,410 --> 00:01:04,530
You don't want to have to
lose a lot of packets.

19
00:01:04,530 --> 00:01:08,430
These are all different
metrics and qualities

20
00:01:08,430 --> 00:01:13,770
that together conform
the quality of service.

21
00:01:13,770 --> 00:01:19,480
Different services will have
different QoS requirements.

22
00:01:20,930 --> 00:01:24,770
How do you go about creating and

23
00:01:24,770 --> 00:01:29,135
enforcing some QoS policy?

24
00:01:29,135 --> 00:01:32,895
There's three main
building blocks.

25
00:01:32,895 --> 00:01:35,700
The first is classification.

26
00:01:35,700 --> 00:01:38,290
This is inspecting packets to

27
00:01:38,290 --> 00:01:42,630
identify what class of
traffic they belong to.

28
00:01:42,710 --> 00:01:45,790
The next is shaping.

29
00:01:45,790 --> 00:01:49,630
This is about ensuring a
given class of traffic

30
00:01:49,630 --> 00:01:54,250
conforms to some desired
properties such as rates.

31
00:01:54,250 --> 00:01:56,565
Then the last is scheduling,

32
00:01:56,565 --> 00:02:00,310
so determining which
packet to transmit next

33
00:02:00,310 --> 00:02:05,100
and which packets to drop.
We'll go through each.

34
00:02:05,360 --> 00:02:08,505
Starting with classification.

35
00:02:08,505 --> 00:02:10,640
Given a packet,

36
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:12,650
we want to place it into

37
00:02:12,650 --> 00:02:16,190
a queue representing
some traffic class.

38
00:02:16,190 --> 00:02:18,910
Maybe this is a low,

39
00:02:18,910 --> 00:02:22,855
medium, and high priority
traffic type classification.

40
00:02:22,855 --> 00:02:24,840
Low priority might be E-mail,

41
00:02:24,840 --> 00:02:26,650
medium priority might be web,

42
00:02:26,650 --> 00:02:28,790
and then high priority traffic

43
00:02:28,790 --> 00:02:31,100
might be video conferencing.

44
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:35,630
There's various mechanisms
that you can go

45
00:02:35,630 --> 00:02:38,935
about classifying this traffic.

46
00:02:38,935 --> 00:02:42,465
One is just based
on packet headers.

47
00:02:42,465 --> 00:02:43,980
We can look at Layer 3 and

48
00:02:43,980 --> 00:02:47,325
4 headers to identify
the traffic.

49
00:02:47,325 --> 00:02:50,810
For example, if we
see TCP port 80,

50
00:02:50,810 --> 00:02:54,070
we know that's web traffic
so we can classify that

51
00:02:54,070 --> 00:02:58,870
as web traffic and put it
into that class of traffic.

52
00:02:58,870 --> 00:03:02,710
Maybe we see specific customers

53
00:03:02,710 --> 00:03:05,640
which we've assigned
IP addresses,

54
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:10,765
maybe the 1.2.3.0/24 range,

55
00:03:10,765 --> 00:03:14,170
maybe they pay more
for their service,

56
00:03:14,170 --> 00:03:18,140
and so we want to put them
in a higher classification.

57
00:03:20,210 --> 00:03:24,765
We could also look based
on deep packet inspection.

58
00:03:24,765 --> 00:03:27,830
That means inspect
the packet payload.

59
00:03:27,830 --> 00:03:29,750
We can look at the payload

60
00:03:29,750 --> 00:03:33,910
for known headers
or bit patterns.

61
00:03:33,910 --> 00:03:38,110
This might look inside to see,

62
00:03:38,110 --> 00:03:40,630
all right, this is particular,

63
00:03:40,630 --> 00:03:43,350
go into specific websites,

64
00:03:43,350 --> 00:03:47,955
look at the HTTP requests
and things like that.

65
00:03:47,955 --> 00:03:50,550
This is computationally
expensive,

66
00:03:50,550 --> 00:03:53,390
but it does help
identify traffic that

67
00:03:53,390 --> 00:03:57,540
can't be identified
at Layer 3 and 4.

68
00:03:57,570 --> 00:04:01,645
Then finally, we could do
this based on fingerprinting,

69
00:04:01,645 --> 00:04:04,600
so protocols, applications,

70
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:06,840
operating systems all have

71
00:04:06,840 --> 00:04:11,150
fingerprints such as the
distribution of packet sizes,

72
00:04:11,150 --> 00:04:15,600
interpacket gaps, and so forth.

73
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:18,770
You can perform
statistical analysis over

74
00:04:18,770 --> 00:04:23,340
many packets and you can
identify traffic that way.

75
00:04:23,340 --> 00:04:26,210
These are all mechanisms that

76
00:04:26,210 --> 00:04:29,370
we have to be able
to classify traffic.

77
00:04:29,410 --> 00:04:33,130
Now, moving on to
traffic shaping.

78
00:04:33,130 --> 00:04:36,630
The goal here is for traffic of

79
00:04:36,630 --> 00:04:40,720
a given class to
conform to some shape.

80
00:04:40,720 --> 00:04:45,290
Two properties that are

81
00:04:45,290 --> 00:04:50,115
related to this are the traffic
rate and the burst rate.

82
00:04:50,115 --> 00:04:54,150
Shown here is the traffic
rate or the allowed rate.

83
00:04:54,150 --> 00:04:57,650
Maybe we want to
say, on average we

84
00:04:57,650 --> 00:05:01,370
want it to conform to
this allowed rate,

85
00:05:01,370 --> 00:05:03,200
don't want it to go over.

86
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:05,810
But we do allow it to have

87
00:05:05,810 --> 00:05:09,170
little bits of time
where it bursts traffic,

88
00:05:09,170 --> 00:05:12,830
especially if it
didn't have that.

89
00:05:12,830 --> 00:05:16,730
The amount of traffic
that raised to

90
00:05:16,730 --> 00:05:20,825
the allowed traffic
level in the past.

91
00:05:20,825 --> 00:05:24,935
This is different
properties we can assign.

92
00:05:24,935 --> 00:05:26,810
How we go about doing that

93
00:05:26,810 --> 00:05:32,780
is using or one

94
00:05:32,780 --> 00:05:36,725
particular method is the
token bucket abstraction,

95
00:05:36,725 --> 00:05:40,340
and this is a way to
implement rate limiting.

96
00:05:40,340 --> 00:05:42,710
In this, we have
this right here,

97
00:05:42,710 --> 00:05:46,250
represents a bucket and we can

98
00:05:46,250 --> 00:05:50,270
place tokens into the bucket at

99
00:05:50,270 --> 00:05:52,310
a given rate R.

100
00:05:52,310 --> 00:05:56,450
Then a packet can

101
00:05:56,450 --> 00:05:59,915
only be transmitted if there's
a token in the bucket,

102
00:05:59,915 --> 00:06:04,460
otherwise, it waits until
a token gets there.

103
00:06:04,460 --> 00:06:07,130
You could see what this
would do would be make it

104
00:06:07,130 --> 00:06:10,730
conform to this specific rate.

105
00:06:10,730 --> 00:06:13,805
Because we get packets
coming in, and has to wait.

106
00:06:13,805 --> 00:06:15,830
As soon as that token drops,

107
00:06:15,830 --> 00:06:20,585
it'll get sent and then it
waits until the next token.

108
00:06:20,585 --> 00:06:23,330
Since the tokens are
being added at rate R,

109
00:06:23,330 --> 00:06:27,480
that's why the packets
conform to that rate.

110
00:06:27,820 --> 00:06:34,595
Now, how handles burst is by
looking at the bucket size.

111
00:06:34,595 --> 00:06:38,165
The bucket size, which
is the depth here,

112
00:06:38,165 --> 00:06:41,570
determines the burst rate.

113
00:06:41,570 --> 00:06:45,170
For example, let's say there's
no traffic for a while.

114
00:06:45,170 --> 00:06:48,830
During this time,
tokens are being

115
00:06:48,830 --> 00:06:53,450
added at rate R and we start
filling up the bucket.

116
00:06:53,450 --> 00:07:00,095
Now, actually these packets
do come and they get there,

117
00:07:00,095 --> 00:07:02,150
when we do get traffic,

118
00:07:02,150 --> 00:07:05,960
which would be a burst
of traffic of packets,

119
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:09,635
there are tokens for
the entire burst.

120
00:07:09,635 --> 00:07:14,420
The bucket would have
enough tokens for that,

121
00:07:14,420 --> 00:07:16,010
it allows the burst,

122
00:07:16,010 --> 00:07:18,230
and then once it empties, well,

123
00:07:18,230 --> 00:07:21,020
then we go back to the rate.

124
00:07:21,020 --> 00:07:27,260
Now, if you want to
constrain the burst size,

125
00:07:27,260 --> 00:07:30,110
well then that's the
depth of the bucket.

126
00:07:30,110 --> 00:07:32,090
Eventually, the bucket will

127
00:07:32,090 --> 00:07:34,325
overflow and you just
start discarding

128
00:07:34,325 --> 00:07:36,290
tokens so you can limit

129
00:07:36,290 --> 00:07:39,930
the burst size by
limiting the bucket size.

130
00:07:41,440 --> 00:07:45,215
Now on to scheduling.

131
00:07:45,215 --> 00:07:50,300
There's two main
functions in scheduling.

132
00:07:50,300 --> 00:07:54,725
One is given a cue that
is starting to fill up,

133
00:07:54,725 --> 00:07:58,880
determine what and when to drop.

134
00:07:58,880 --> 00:08:01,340
Then given multiple cues,

135
00:08:01,340 --> 00:08:05,010
determine which packet
to transmit next.

136
00:08:05,500 --> 00:08:08,645
Let's talk about
queues real quick.

137
00:08:08,645 --> 00:08:11,525
We've shown the generic
router architecture,

138
00:08:11,525 --> 00:08:14,310
has these packet buffers.

139
00:08:14,890 --> 00:08:18,290
We've seen in the past.

140
00:08:18,290 --> 00:08:20,660
We've talked about congestion.

141
00:08:20,660 --> 00:08:23,820
If you get multiple,

142
00:08:27,280 --> 00:08:30,125
let's say it's a
four port router,

143
00:08:30,125 --> 00:08:32,270
three of the ports are receiving

144
00:08:32,270 --> 00:08:34,460
packets and they're
all going to be

145
00:08:34,460 --> 00:08:38,540
transmitted out of
the fourth port so

146
00:08:38,540 --> 00:08:41,810
the queues would start filling

147
00:08:41,810 --> 00:08:46,590
up because you can't
transmit fast enough.

148
00:08:47,320 --> 00:08:50,975
When we have these cues
or packet buffers,

149
00:08:50,975 --> 00:08:53,870
if we create really big ones,

150
00:08:53,870 --> 00:08:57,455
beyond the concern of how
much memory it will take,

151
00:08:57,455 --> 00:09:01,340
well, then that's creating
these really long delays.

152
00:09:01,340 --> 00:09:05,480
Let's say we put a packet in,

153
00:09:05,480 --> 00:09:09,050
it's going to have to wait
this whole time to get

154
00:09:09,050 --> 00:09:12,905
through the entire queue
before it's transmitted.

155
00:09:12,905 --> 00:09:14,660
This induces a lot of

156
00:09:14,660 --> 00:09:17,345
latency and maybe that's
not what we want.

157
00:09:17,345 --> 00:09:19,460
If we do it too short,

158
00:09:19,460 --> 00:09:22,280
well this is going to
lead to a lot of drops.

159
00:09:22,280 --> 00:09:23,735
Let's say we only add

160
00:09:23,735 --> 00:09:29,405
two slots for packets
and two arrive,

161
00:09:29,405 --> 00:09:31,595
well, you're going to be
dropping one of them.

162
00:09:31,595 --> 00:09:35,130
That's not exactly great either.

163
00:09:36,010 --> 00:09:39,935
This is fields of
study on its own

164
00:09:39,935 --> 00:09:44,100
to figure out what the
good buffer sizes are.

165
00:09:44,140 --> 00:09:47,045
Now, there's other
things we can do

166
00:09:47,045 --> 00:09:50,195
algorithmically at run time

167
00:09:50,195 --> 00:09:53,600
to determine things
like what to drop.

168
00:09:53,600 --> 00:09:57,815
The simplest one is a tail drop.

169
00:09:57,815 --> 00:09:59,885
When the queue fills up,

170
00:09:59,885 --> 00:10:02,540
just drop whatever packets

171
00:10:02,540 --> 00:10:06,000
can't be in drop from the tail.

172
00:10:06,400 --> 00:10:13,430
But the problem with tail
drop is, let's consider TCP.

173
00:10:13,430 --> 00:10:18,380
It uses packet loss as an
indication of congestion,

174
00:10:18,380 --> 00:10:21,545
such that it then slows
its sending rate.

175
00:10:21,545 --> 00:10:24,515
Now let's imagine
we're using tail-drop.

176
00:10:24,515 --> 00:10:27,335
As our inside of the network,

177
00:10:27,335 --> 00:10:30,200
the queue gets all congested,

178
00:10:30,200 --> 00:10:33,005
filled up and then
we drop a packet.

179
00:10:33,005 --> 00:10:35,780
With tail-drop, packet
loss would occur

180
00:10:35,780 --> 00:10:38,690
when congestion
reached its peak.

181
00:10:38,690 --> 00:10:42,650
Then we need a
round trip time or

182
00:10:42,650 --> 00:10:47,240
a timeout amount of time
for when TCP would say,

183
00:10:47,240 --> 00:10:48,710
I think there's congestion,

184
00:10:48,710 --> 00:10:52,055
or detect that
there's packet loss.

185
00:10:52,055 --> 00:10:54,860
In the meantime,
there's going to be

186
00:10:54,860 --> 00:11:00,180
many packets that were
sent and likely dropped.

187
00:11:00,430 --> 00:11:04,655
There's a lot of
other approaches

188
00:11:04,655 --> 00:11:07,700
that have been introduced over

189
00:11:07,700 --> 00:11:11,240
the years that effectively drop

190
00:11:11,240 --> 00:11:15,275
packets earlier to
signal to CCP earlier.

191
00:11:15,275 --> 00:11:17,930
It's doing things
probabilistic-ally,

192
00:11:17,930 --> 00:11:20,015
like it's starting to fill up.

193
00:11:20,015 --> 00:11:23,450
Well, maybe let's
probabilistically drop a packet,

194
00:11:23,450 --> 00:11:25,610
but not drop all of the packets,

195
00:11:25,610 --> 00:11:30,015
and so this will start
signaling to them.

196
00:11:30,015 --> 00:11:32,270
I highly encourage
you to go read

197
00:11:32,270 --> 00:11:34,760
about these different ones

198
00:11:34,760 --> 00:11:42,270
and they're much more
widely used today.

199
00:11:42,730 --> 00:11:49,835
The second part is what
queue to transmit from.

200
00:11:49,835 --> 00:11:52,595
Let's say we've done
our classification.

201
00:11:52,595 --> 00:11:54,980
We've already done shaping,
which is not shown here,

202
00:11:54,980 --> 00:11:58,625
but we put into these
different classes of traffic.

203
00:11:58,625 --> 00:12:04,295
Now, which do we actually
transmit on the wire next?

204
00:12:04,295 --> 00:12:07,640
We could go with
strict priority.

205
00:12:07,640 --> 00:12:11,135
If the top queue has
a packet to send,

206
00:12:11,135 --> 00:12:13,940
send that, otherwise
look at the next

207
00:12:13,940 --> 00:12:17,840
one and send that, and so forth.

208
00:12:17,840 --> 00:12:20,120
Now, this has a problem
that it could completely

209
00:12:20,120 --> 00:12:23,045
starve out the lower priorities.

210
00:12:23,045 --> 00:12:25,760
Other approaches might

211
00:12:25,760 --> 00:12:28,325
include something
like round-robin.

212
00:12:28,325 --> 00:12:31,460
We saw that with load balancing,

213
00:12:31,460 --> 00:12:33,215
but this is a different context.

214
00:12:33,215 --> 00:12:36,590
This is cycling
through each queue,

215
00:12:36,590 --> 00:12:39,680
so we transmit one packet
from the top queue,

216
00:12:39,680 --> 00:12:41,390
one packet from
the middle queue,

217
00:12:41,390 --> 00:12:45,200
one packet from the bottom
queue, and so forth.

218
00:12:45,200 --> 00:12:47,660
This is not necessarily

219
00:12:47,660 --> 00:12:51,350
using the queues as
a priority list,

220
00:12:51,350 --> 00:12:54,260
but putting them in for
different properties,

221
00:12:54,260 --> 00:12:57,840
so probably were
shaped differently.

222
00:12:57,850 --> 00:13:01,910
Then a problem with that is

223
00:13:01,910 --> 00:13:07,460
packet sizes aren't
necessarily exactly the same,

224
00:13:07,460 --> 00:13:10,505
there's a variance on that,

225
00:13:10,505 --> 00:13:13,010
something called fair queuing.

226
00:13:13,010 --> 00:13:17,900
It tries to mimic a
bit-per-bit multiplexing.

227
00:13:17,900 --> 00:13:20,405
Imagine if we could take
one bit from this queue,

228
00:13:20,405 --> 00:13:21,845
one bit from this queue,

229
00:13:21,845 --> 00:13:23,780
one bit from the bottom queue,

230
00:13:23,780 --> 00:13:26,525
and then do
round-robin that way,

231
00:13:26,525 --> 00:13:29,825
that will be the most fair.

232
00:13:29,825 --> 00:13:33,125
It's not practical because
you can't actually do that,

233
00:13:33,125 --> 00:13:36,500
but it mimics that approach.

234
00:13:36,500 --> 00:13:39,080
It does that multiplexing by

235
00:13:39,080 --> 00:13:42,470
computing the theoretical
departure date for each packet

236
00:13:42,470 --> 00:13:45,260
and then using that as
part of the algorithm

237
00:13:45,260 --> 00:13:49,410
to determine what
to transmit next.

238
00:13:49,480 --> 00:13:53,540
Let's recap on QoS.

239
00:13:53,540 --> 00:13:55,865
We have the classification,

240
00:13:55,865 --> 00:13:57,800
which is inspecting packets to

241
00:13:57,800 --> 00:14:00,635
identify what class of
traffic they belong to.

242
00:14:00,635 --> 00:14:03,590
They then get shaped,

243
00:14:03,590 --> 00:14:06,680
which ensures a given
class of traffic

244
00:14:06,680 --> 00:14:09,530
conforms to the desired
properties such as rates.

245
00:14:09,530 --> 00:14:11,270
And then we have scheduling,

246
00:14:11,270 --> 00:14:12,830
which is determining which

247
00:14:12,830 --> 00:14:18,785
packet to transmit next
and which packets to drop.

248
00:14:18,785 --> 00:14:26,990
One last note is that all of
this is on the egress side.

249
00:14:26,990 --> 00:14:29,060
On the ingress side,

250
00:14:29,060 --> 00:14:31,685
there's something
called policing,

251
00:14:31,685 --> 00:14:34,760
which is effectively
the same as shaping,

252
00:14:34,760 --> 00:14:38,240
like you want to be able
to enforce a rate limit.

253
00:14:38,240 --> 00:14:40,100
But the difference is it can

254
00:14:40,100 --> 00:14:42,125
only take one corrective action,

255
00:14:42,125 --> 00:14:44,240
which is dropping
a received packet.

256
00:14:44,240 --> 00:14:47,510
Let's say we have a host and
we're receiving traffic.

257
00:14:47,510 --> 00:14:50,510
Well, you can't really do much
with it other than drop at

258
00:14:50,510 --> 00:14:53,570
that point. So that's policing.

259
00:14:53,570 --> 00:14:55,490
Just wanted to mention that,

260
00:14:55,490 --> 00:14:58,790
but we've now gone
